<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louise of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a>–<a href="/years/1810">1810</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Queen Louise of Prussia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louise of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> (1776–1810) was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> as the wife of Frederick William III. After Prussia's defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1806 she met him at Tilsit in 1807 to plead for better terms. Her early death made her a national heroine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and her son became Emperor William I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louise-of-mecklenburg-strelitz.jpg" alt="Louise of Mecklenburg-Strelitz" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Grassi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grassi,_Josef_Mathias_-_Luise_von_Mecklenburg-Strelitz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louise was born on 10 March 1776 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, the fourth daughter of Duke Charles of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>, a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Queen Charlotte</a>, and of Princess Friederike of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>. Her mother died in childbirth when Louise was six, and after the early death of her stepmother the children were placed in the care of their grandmother Princess Maria Louise at Darmstadt. A governess from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> taught them French, in which Louise became fluent while neglecting German. She learned to love German literature, above all Schiller. In 1793 she met the Prussian crown prince Frederick William at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, and he at once chose her as his wife. They were married on 24 December 1793.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The couple preferred to live at Paretz Palace, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, and had nine children who survived infancy, including the future Frederick William IV and the future William I. On 16 November 1797 her husband became King Frederick William III. Louise became a public figure in her own right, which was new in Prussian history, and the king consulted her on affairs of state. She supported his neutrality in the early years of the wars against revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> but later became an open advocate of war.</p><p>After the Prussian disaster at Jena-Auerstedt in October 1806 the royal family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> dictated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Peace of Tilsit</a> in 1807, the king sent for Louise to beg for better terms, but when she met Napoleon at Tilsit he made no concessions. Napoleon had tried to destroy her reputation, but his charges only made her more beloved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The terms imposed an indemnity of one hundred and twenty million francs, equal to the whole yearly budget of Prussia. Louise supported the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-friedrich-karl-vom-stein">Baron vom Stein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-august-von-hardenberg">Karl August von Hardenberg</a>, and she helped bring about Stein's reappointment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louise died on 19 July 1810, aged thirty-four, in her husband's arms while visiting her father at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>. The cause was an unidentified illness, which her subjects attributed to the French occupation. She was buried at Charlottenburg Palace, where a mausoleum with a recumbent statue by Christian Daniel Rauch was built over her grave. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> is reported to have said that the king had lost his best minister.</p><p>She was revered as the soul of national virtue. In 1814 her husband founded the Order of Louise as a counterpart to the Iron Cross, and a statue of her was erected in the Tiergarten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1880. In the 1920s conservative women founded the Queen Louise League, which the Nazis disbanded in 1934.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louise of Mecklenburg-Strelitz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charlotte of Mecklenburg-Strelitz married George III of Great Britain in 1761, and her niece <b>Louise</b> became Queen of Prussia and a national heroine.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Schloss Hohenzieritz, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.37,9.74&amp;year=1798" aria-label="Hanover on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louise of Mecklenburg-Strelitz"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5506 5613h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5506 5613h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.37,9.74&amp;year=1798">What was here in 1798? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
